{
  "analysis": "The review attributes the described problems to bugs, crashing, lag, freezing, and unresponsive controls. The gift opening and trading behavior is not described as a social obligation or unsolicited messaging. The PvP and Team GO Rocket battles explicitly involve fighting an opponent, which describes an in-game competition mechanic; the technical failures themselves do not trigger dark-pattern labels under R11.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the pvp & rockets battles are also lagging switching out are too slow, charge attacks are not responding well and my game freeze while the opponent keep attacking",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11 excludes the bug-related behavior but does not suppress the underlying competition mechanic",
      "rationale": "PvP and battles against an opponent explicitly describe players competing against other players or opponents."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a competitive player-versus-player card game in which players face opponents using recurring card combinations, including Mega Knight, PEKKA, and Golem. It explicitly claims that spending money provides an advantage and that advancing higher is not achievable through skill alone, which supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. The complaints about annoying cards and strategies are evaluations of gameplay balance, not separate dark-pattern mechanics. The mention of updates and card buffs does not establish that purchased items lose value over time, so Power Creep is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly states that payment provides an advantage and that progression to higher levels cannot be achieved through skill alone."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition and indicator: the game makes you compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against other players using particular card combinations."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a pass that the player can buy, which is an existing battle-pass mechanic and therefore supports Recurring Fee under the codebook. It also mentions friends, a team, and trading lives, but does not describe inviting friends, an obligation to keep playing for teammates, or feeling required to reciprocate, so no social label is supported. The statement that the player does not have to pay to win explicitly denies a pay-to-win mechanic rather than describing one. The diamonds and the game element are discussed in the context of customer-service resolution and do not establish a qualifying currency, gambling, or other dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes [indicators] under M_RecurringFee",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly refers to an in-game pass that can be purchased, which is a battle-pass mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current time-based mechanics despite presenting them as suggestions. The game makes the player wait for a new life, implying an in-game timer that delays continued play. It also provides daily prizes that reset when a day is missed, motivating daily return and penalizing absence. The harder levels and celebratory tasks are mentioned, but they do not clearly establish grinding or another coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.",
      "rationale": "The suggestion presupposes that obtaining a new life currently requires waiting, indicating a timer-gated delay before the player can continue."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars and darkpattern.games' definition: encourages return visits every day and punishes missing a day.",
      "rationale": "The game gives daily prizes and resets progress in that reward sequence when the player misses a day."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes building and expanding an empire through strategy and battling other players in real-time combat. The real-time battles explicitly establish player-versus-player competition. No other described mechanic matches a listed dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the game makes players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes real-time combat between the reviewer and other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progression being blocked by the game's energy system unless they make a purchase. The statement that purchasing is necessary to make any progress is an explicit payment gate. The review does not describe timers, daily login incentives, advertisements, or a specific currency exchange, so no other labels are supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "definition: Patterns that require the players to spend money to progress in-game; also matches the Pay Wall indicator because further progress is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that purchasing is necessary to make progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits play: after the player runs out of energy, they cannot meaningfully progress until the recovery timer completes. This is an in-game timer imposing a wait before further progress, which matches Playing by Appointment. The requests for a console version, a storyline, bigger maps, and more character customization are suggestions about absent features and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers; darkpattern.games: in-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "The energy system blocks further progress until a long in-game recovery timer finishes."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a consumable-item purchase loop: after purchasing Poké Balls, the reviewer says captures fail more often, causing the balls to be depleted faster and creating a need to purchase additional balls. This explicitly connects spending money on items with continuing capture-related progress. The later statement disputes the authorship of the review and the claimed conversations with other players, but it does not identify a technical malfunction in the game itself, so the described purchase mechanic remains codable.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes needing to buy additional Poké Balls to continue making captures, making spending a condition for capture-related progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progression being blocked by difficult levels that can take a week to clear, and tasks that the player says are impossible without purchasing boosters. The time claim alone does not explicitly describe repetitive or tedious task completion, so it does not establish Grinding or Playing by Appointment. The explicit payment gate does establish Pay to Progress because boosters are required to overcome the tasks and continue progressing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review says progress through the tasks is effectively unavailable unless the player spends money on boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited event with a promised reward, partial progress toward that reward, and a visible countdown saying there were still two days remaining. After the player stopped playing and logged back in after sleeping, the event had been replaced despite the displayed time. This creates pressure to keep playing or risk missing the reward, supporting Fear of Missing Out. The event’s apparent limited availability and premature replacement also describe manufactured scarcity or urgency, supporting Deceptive Luxury. The review does not explicitly describe daily login rewards, repetitive grinding, payment requirements, or a mandatory session.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; indicator: patterns that induce anxiety that players will miss exciting or important events if they stop playing",
      "rationale": "The player had nearly completed a reward-bearing event but feared losing the reward after stopping play, and the event changed before the displayed two-day period ended."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity: limited-time offers or events with unnecessary urgency",
      "rationale": "The game presented the event as available for two more days but replaced it after the player briefly stopped playing, creating manufactured scarcity around the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a friend-invitation mechanic tied to rewards: the player invites friends and gets them to join Facebook in exchange for in-game rewards, although the reviewer says those rewards have stopped appearing after the new season. This uses the player's social connections to recruit others, supporting Forced Fellowship. The lack of dice, unsuccessful shut downs and bank heists, duplicate stickers, and the connection error do not by themselves establish another coded mechanic: the latter is explicitly a technical fault, and the review does not clearly state that real money is used for gambling or that the random outcomes come from a defined reward system.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme: patterns that reward players for inviting friends or encourage/force them to invite people to join the game to gain in-game incentives.",
      "rationale": "The game links in-game rewards to recruiting friends and getting them to join Facebook, using the player's social connections as a recruitment mechanism."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game interface that automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-stakes table whenever the game opens or the player enters the 1v1 section. This design directs the player toward the most expensive option and makes an accidental selection more likely, while the reviewer says the consequence is losing coins and then buying more currency with real money. The requested Default Table Lock and lowest-bet default are proposed fixes, not existing mechanics, so they are not coded. The accidental loss is of in-game coins rather than a real-money purchase, so M_EasyToPurchase is not assigned. The clearest supported pattern is UI Misdirection because the interface purposefully steers attention and selection toward the highest-bet table.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically focuses and defaults the player onto the highest-cost betting option instead of leaving the player at a neutral or low-stakes choice."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a repetitive base-building experience, calling it a grinder and saying it is the same old pattern. It also describes an event in which ordinary players cannot win, while players can spend very large amounts of money to win; this is a competitive event whose outcome is influenced by spending. The review does not describe a payment gate required for ordinary progression, only spending for competitive advantage, so the supported monetary label is Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. No specific timer, daily login requirement, advertisement, collection mechanic, or technical malfunction is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Same old same old grinder",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Calling the game a grinder describes repetitive, tedious gameplay used to make progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review describes players competing in an event with a winner."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes spending money to obtain an advantage and win in the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. The game grants advantages through spending, explicitly framing progression as pay to win. Base upgrades use increasingly long timers, imposing waits on development. Progression continually scales without an apparent endpoint and requires more repetitive grinding. The interface is described as deliberately confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, which supports UI Misdirection because the interface is being used to steer players toward buying. Alliance participation is effectively coerced: stronger alliances dictate development choices and players outside alliances are attacked and bullied, making social affiliation necessary for safety and progress. The review does not explicitly describe daily rewards, gambling, a recurring fee, or a technical download deception.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that spending provides an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits",
      "rationale": "Base updates are governed by increasingly long timers, requiring the player to wait before development completes."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "definition: performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly characterizes continued progress as requiring grind."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that continually expand the game to never allow players to complete",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling implies an indefinitely expanding progression system without a meaningful completion point."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "definition: the user interface contains elements designed to purposefully focus attention on specific options or away from opting out",
      "rationale": "The interface is intentionally made confusing and overwhelming to steer players toward purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Social Obligation / Guilds indicator: design that makes players feel compelled to participate in a group to avoid harm or letting others down",
      "rationale": "Being outside an alliance exposes the player to attacks and bullying, coercing alliance membership as a condition of safe participation."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an existing daily-reward system that requires the player to bet 50,000 in order to complete and claim the daily rewards. It also mentions timed rewards, but does not describe an arbitrary waiting timer that blocks play. The statement about not paying is the reviewer’s refusal, not evidence that payment is required, so no monetary label applies. The 50,000 betting requirement is a progress task, but the review does not establish repetitive or tedious activity strongly enough for Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in/task rewards indicator; definition",
      "rationale": "The game provides daily rewards tied to completion of a required in-game condition, and the reviewer cannot obtain them without meeting that condition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a sticker-collection progression system in which completing the collection is the player's goal, but the final one or two stickers are effectively withheld unless the player pays a large amount. It also broadly states that winning requires spending hundreds of dollars, indicating a monetary advantage or progression requirement. The statement that a goal succeeds only about one time in a hundred suggests a very low success rate, but it does not clearly identify a chance-based paid mechanic or an unpredictable reward structure, so Gambling and Reward Mania are not assigned. The review's comment about giving five stars concerns the review interface rather than a game mechanic and is out of scope.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator and definition: patterns that grant advantages or progress to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly says that winning in the game requires spending hundreds of dollars."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Progress: further progress or completion is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The final stickers, and therefore completion of the collection, are described as unavailable unless the player pays a large amount."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "Complete the Collection definition: the compulsion to acquire all the items in a game.",
      "rationale": "The review identifies obtaining the final stickers as the relevant completion goal, showing pursuit of a complete item collection."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an existing competitive mechanic in which another player can challenge the reviewer to a game. The sticker-trading feature is requested rather than described as currently present, so it is excluded under R12. The purchased booster and the claimed inability to use it during a challenged game describe a restriction or desired change, but they do not explicitly establish a coded dark-pattern mechanic such as pay-to-progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "if another player challenge me to a game",
      "rule_applied": "definition: “The game makes you compete against other players.”",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes a game in which players challenge one another to compete."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a level-based game with limited lives: the player runs out of lives several times each day while trying to clear levels, and progression is very slow, averaging 5–7 days per level. The central monetization mechanic is that the reviewer says add-ons must be bought to continue through the levels, making payment a condition of progress. The review does not explicitly describe repetitive tasks, scheduled play requirements, a timer-based wait, or a mechanic that makes leaving difficult because of prior investment; statements about frustration, time waste, and dissatisfaction alone do not establish those labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that purchasing add-ons is necessary to get through the game's levels, making payment a condition of progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review explicitly describes the installed game as a strategy game requiring army creation, training, building, and resource gathering, with the advertised-style puzzles appearing only occasionally. The work is framed as chores required to reach those puzzles, which describes repetitive tasks performed for access or progress and supports Grinding. The misleading advertising itself is out of scope under R13, so it does not receive a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "you'll be doing chores in order to get to them",
      "rule_applied": "definition; repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress in a game",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes chores as required work before reaching the puzzle content, implying tedious progression tasks rather than direct access to the desired gameplay."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertised pin-pull mini-games as rare and says the reviewer paid real money for extra coins and other items while trying to encounter them. The payment for coins explicitly describes exchanging real-world money for in-game currency, which supports Intermediate Currency. The pin-pull claims are advertising or alleged misrepresentation, but the codebook excludes out-of-game marketing and has no general false-advertising label. The high level and time investment do not by themselves establish grinding or difficulty leaving. The connection problem after required updates is attributed to a technical fault, so it does not trigger a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "I have also paid a lot for extra coins and things",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes paying real-world money to acquire extra in-game coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several current in-game monetization mechanics. Progress is presented as effectively requiring payment, and a newer bot must be bought with money rather than earned, which supports Pay to Progress. The game repeatedly places in-app purchase offers on the screen at login before play, but the review does not describe a specific purchase-by-default, accidental-purchase, or attention-directing interface behavior, so Easy to Purchase and UI Misdirection are not supported. It also says free-to-play rewards are obtained through ads, indicating incentivized advertisements. The reference to a shifting meta and having to buy the newest bot suggests changing competitive viability, but it does not explicitly say that previously purchased items lose value over time, so Power Creep is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck! Even if you wanted to do f2p, you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay to Progress includes requiring or encouraging players to spend money to make progress, including Pay to Win and Pay Wall indicators.",
      "rationale": "The review states that progression and keeping up with the game's meta require purchasing the newest bot with real money rather than earning it."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rule_applied": "indicator: forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads.",
      "rationale": "The span links free-to-play rewards to advertisements, implying that ads are viewed or otherwise used as the condition for receiving those rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a card-collection system in which players gather cards toward completing sets. Near the end of a set's availability, new cards allegedly stop appearing, so the player can collect many cards yet fail to complete the set and lose its completion reward. The collection goal supports Complete the Collection. The expiring set and the explicitly missed reward also create pressure through fear of missing out. The review does not describe payment, currency, gambling, or a required play schedule beyond the limited collection period.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "after one is almost close to completing a set, new cards don't appear again",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the player is pursuing completion of a card set",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes collecting cards to complete a set and treats failure to obtain the remaining cards as the central problem."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "especially when the set is almost expiring. So after collecting 125 cards from the beginning of the collection you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "definition; fear of missing rewards or falling behind when an event or opportunity ends",
      "rationale": "The expiring set creates pressure because the player may miss the completion reward despite substantial progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a capped inventory system that prevents continued play because board and refrigerator slots are occupied by generator parts and unfinished items. They are unwilling to delete valuable or partially completed items, so the limited storage creates pressure either to discard items or spend real money to buy more parts. This directly matches Waste Aversion. The statement that they cannot play without spending money also describes payment being used to continue progress, supporting Pay to Progress. The review does not establish a collection-completion goal or a currency-exchange mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "Not enough slots to store and play without spending $$ to buy parts.",
      "rule_applied": "Indicator: \"Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory.\"",
      "rationale": "Limited storage makes continued play difficult and pressures the player to discard items or spend money to deal with the inventory cap."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "store and play without spending $$ to buy parts",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that require players to spend money to progress in-game.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer presents spending real money on parts as necessary to keep playing at the higher level."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repeatedly playing bingo rounds to collect photos for a set, which is a collection-completion goal reached through repetitive play. After losing a round, the game presents an offer to spend extra coins to obtain a missing photo; however, the reviewer says that spending the coins does not produce the promised photo. The review does not clearly establish that the coins are bought with real money, that the offer is chance-based, or that the player is required to pay to continue, so monetary labels are not assigned. The failed offer is described as a deceptive or malfunctioning outcome, but no available label directly covers a broken promise of this kind.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I play round after round to collect 'photos'",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes performing repeated bingo rounds in order to make progress toward collecting photos."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "to complete the set",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game goal is to acquire missing photos and complete an in-game collection."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repetitive resource collection as the main route to leveling: the player is “grinding resources” so they can level up and obtain more attempts at another game. It also describes alliances attacking one another, specifically larger alliances attacking smaller ones for resources, which is direct player-versus-player competition. The reference to dedicating time to the game and alliances expresses investment and disappointment, but does not explicitly say that leaving would forfeit accumulated value or let friends down, so it does not independently support Easy to Get, Hard to Lose or Forced Fellowship. The mention of an advertisement is not a clear description of an in-game mechanic and is outside scope if it refers to marketing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "It's just grinding resources trying not to get raided so you can level up",
      "rule_applied": "definition; repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes repeatedly collecting resources to make leveling progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "The bigger alliances all just attack the smaller alliances and take their resources.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the game makes players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "Alliances compete directly by attacking other alliances and taking their resources."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
